      315 heading The problem
      317 paragraph
        318 text A phone that would not stop ringing. The hotel had no website. Guests could not see rooms or prices for themselves, so every question landed on the front desk: the same few answers, all day long.
      319 heading What existed before
      321 paragraph
        322 text Word of mouth and WhatsApp statuses. Rooms, prices, and directions lived in conversations. Every enquiry started from zero, and nothing could be checked before calling.
      323 heading What I changed
      325 paragraph
        326 text One place for everything a guest asks. Photos of every room, prices in plain view, Google Maps directions, and one-tap buttons to call or message on WhatsApp. It opens quickly even on slow mobile data.
      327 heading Technical decisions
      329 paragraph
        330 text Information, not transactions. The hotel takes bookings on WhatsApp, so the site’s job is to inform, not to process payments. I kept it a light hand-built site with compressed images and no heavy booking platform, so it loads fast on the networks guests actually use.
      331 heading What changed after launch
      333 paragraph
        334 text Guests arrive informed. The site put rooms, prices, directions, and WhatsApp contact in one place, so guests could answer many of their own pre-booking questions before calling the front desk. Booking conversations start with the details already known.
      335 heading What I would improve next
      337 paragraph
        338 text An availability calendar. The next useful step is showing open dates per room, so guests can check availability before they message. That is the improvement I would make next.
