      378 heading The problem
      380 paragraph
        381 text Selling through WhatsApp statuses. Is this in stock? How much with delivery? Every sale started with the same long back-and-forth, typed out again and again.
      382 heading What existed before
      384 paragraph
        385 text Prices and stock lived in chat. Customers could only see what the latest status post showed. Nothing was searchable, and every order needed a conversation to complete.
      386 heading What I changed
      388 paragraph
        389 text A store built for phones. Products sorted into groups with clear descriptions, prices you do not have to ask about, offers in one place, and a cart and checkout that work on the network most customers actually have.
      390 heading Technical decisions
      392 paragraph
        393 text A custom store, not a rented platform. I built the catalogue, cart, and accounts as one light JavaScript app instead of stacking plugins on a heavy platform. Fewer moving parts means fewer things to break on a slow connection, and the shop keeps full control of its products and prices.
      394 heading What changed after launch
      396 paragraph
        397 text Orders that arrive complete. The store gave the business a searchable product catalogue and a structured order flow instead of relying only on WhatsApp status posts. Orders arrive with the items and the delivery address already written down.
      398 heading What I would improve next
      400 paragraph
        401 text Payment at checkout. Taking payment online in the same place the order is placed would close the last gap between browsing and buying. That is the next improvement I would build.
